# The coordinator's credential gates, in one file (issues #249, #247, ADR-0072).
#
# Copy to /etc/bacchus/coordinator-gates.env on the coordinator box (mode 0600).
# deploy/install.sh places a copy of this template on a fresh coordinator; on an
# existing box, put it there by hand. Only this .example is tracked — `*.env` is
# gitignored, with `!*.env.example` re-including this file.
#
# ===========================================================================
# WHAT THIS FILE IS FOR
# ===========================================================================
# Every credential gate this coordinator has FAILS OPEN when its flag is unset:
# -admission-pubkey, -admission-authority, -device-root-pubkey,
# -revocations-root-pubkey and both -*-revocations-source. Each has its reasons
# (ADR-0045 §2 for the sharpest), and the consequence is that an unconfigured
# deployment is indistinguishable from a healthy one — the pin passes, the fleet
# check passes, the probe passes, and nothing is refused anywhere because nothing
# was asked to refuse.
#
# So the gates are a configuration an operator applies DELIBERATELY, and it is a
# file rather than a paragraph. A paragraph rots against cmd/coordinator's flag
# table with nothing to catch it; deploy/gate_check_test.go runs every recipe
# below through the real binary, so a renamed or re-scoped flag turns this file
# red instead of turning a deployment quietly open.
#
# ===========================================================================
# HOW IT REACHES THE PROCESS, AND WHY IT IS ONE VARIABLE
# ===========================================================================
# bacchus-coordinator.service carries two directives for this:
#
#     EnvironmentFile=-/etc/bacchus/coordinator-gates.env
#     ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/bacchus-coordinator … $BACCHUS_COORDINATOR_GATES
#
# `$VAR` — NOT `${VAR}`. systemd splits `$VAR` at whitespace into zero or more
# arguments and expands `${VAR}` into exactly one, always. That difference is the
# whole mechanism:
#
#   * unset or empty gives ZERO arguments, so a box with no gates file runs
#     exactly as it does today. Gates-off stays the default and stays silent.
#   * one variable carrying the whole flag set means the NEXT gate flag is an
#     edit to this file and never an edit to the unit. deploy/bacchus-pin.sh
#     refuses to copy a .service file over one carrying hand-added flags
#     (ADR-0064 §7) precisely because those accumulate, so the unit is the worst
#     place to keep configuration that changes.
#   * `${VAR}`, or one `${VAR}` per flag, cannot express "off": an empty
#     `${…}` still produces an argument, and `-admission-authority ""` is a fatal
#     parse error rather than a disabled gate.
#
# There is no variable expansion inside this file. systemd's env-file parser does
# not resolve `$OTHER_VAR`, so each recipe below is one literal, complete line.
#
# FILL EVERY <PLACEHOLDER> BEFORE UNCOMMENTING A LINE. An unfilled one is not a
# disabled gate: `-admission-pubkey <OPERATOR_ADMISSION_PUBKEY>` is a fatal
# startup error, and behind Restart=always that is a crash loop rather than a
# single clean failure. `systemctl status bacchus-coordinator` names it.
#
# ===========================================================================
# THE PATHS MUST BE ABSOLUTE (issue #247)
# ===========================================================================
# Nine of cmd/coordinator's flags default to a RELATIVE `secrets/…` path. For a
# system service with no WorkingDirectory= that is `/`, so those defaults resolve
# under the root directory where nothing is staged — and a missing revocation
# file is not an error, it means NOTHING IS REVOKED. The unit now ships
# `WorkingDirectory=/etc/bacchus`; every path below is absolute anyway, because a
# gate that depends on a working directory being right is a gate that will be
# wrong on the next box.
#
# Check the result rather than assuming it. The coordinator states every resolved
# path at startup:
#
#     journalctl -u bacchus-coordinator | grep 'paths:'
#     journalctl -u bacchus-coordinator --since -10min | sh deploy/bacchus-gate-check.sh
#
# ===========================================================================
# GATES OFF — what this ships as, and what the testbed runs today (#249)
# ===========================================================================
# Leave this line as it is until the step below it has been staged. An empty
# value is the same as no file at all: zero arguments, no behaviour change.
BACCHUS_COORDINATOR_GATES=

# ===========================================================================
# STEP 1 — admission, and two revocation lists that exist
# ===========================================================================
# Everything in this step can be staged on the testbed TODAY. Nothing in it waits
# on another repository.
#
# Before making it the active line:
#
#   1. Have an admission authority key. One is already staged on the testbed from
#      2026-08-05 (#251) — adopt it rather than minting a second, and note that
#      it currently sits on an EXIT box, which is the wrong box for it (#60's
#      co-location objection, applied to a signing key). `admission-issue
#      -pubkey` prints the public half of whichever key it is given.
#   2. Create both revocation lists, even empty:
#
#        printf '[]\n' > /etc/bacchus/admission-revocations.json
#        printf '[]\n' > /etc/bacchus/device-revocations.json
#        chmod 0600 /etc/bacchus/*-revocations.json
#
#      An ABSENT file is not an error and not a refusal — it is an empty list,
#      which is why #173's revocation loop cannot currently fail. Both files are
#      hot-reloaded, so `admission-issue -revoke <serial>` takes effect with no
#      restart.
#   3. Issue credentials BEFORE restarting the coordinator. Admission applies to
#      nodes as well as clients: a coordinator with an anchor and a fleet without
#      credentials is a fleet that cannot register. See docs/RUNNING.md, "Node
#      admission (who may join)".
#
# -admission-pubkey anchors ONE authority for EVERY role, which is correct while
# the operator mints everything by hand and is the only shape the testbed can run
# until an account service exists. ADR-0047's split — relay,exit on the operator
# key, client on the account service key — is step 2's line, and it is additive:
# nothing else changes when it arrives.
#
#BACCHUS_COORDINATOR_GATES="-admission-pubkey <OPERATOR_ADMISSION_PUBKEY> -admission-revocations /etc/bacchus/admission-revocations.json -device-revocations /etc/bacchus/device-revocations.json"

# ===========================================================================
# STEP 2 — the device entitlement gate, and the account service's address
# ===========================================================================
# BLOCKED on bacchus-payment#82: a deployed account service. Both flags name it.
#
# -device-root-pubkey is the OFFLINE ROOT the account service's device-credential
# chain anchors to — not the admission authority's key, and not something to
# invent locally. With no service minting device credentials, turning this on
# refuses every client, so the gate and its issuer arrive together or not at all.
# It also requires an audience: the unit's -advertise supplies it.
#
# -account-service publishes the service's address in the signed cold-start
# directory so a client can follow it when it moves (#209, ADR-0061). Repeat the
# flag, in preference order, for every address a client should try — the
# directory's list REPLACES a client's configured one, so naming a single address
# narrows a client that had two. `https` only, scheme and host, no path.
#
# The role split lands here too, because it is what makes the always-online
# issuer unable to admit a relay or an exit (ADR-0047, and bacchus-payment#38's
# 2026-08-09 ruling that the admission authority is a separate key held by the
# account service).
#
#BACCHUS_COORDINATOR_GATES="-admission-authority relay,exit:<OPERATOR_ADMISSION_PUBKEY> -admission-authority client:<ACCOUNT_SERVICE_ADMISSION_PUBKEY> -admission-revocations /etc/bacchus/admission-revocations.json -device-revocations /etc/bacchus/device-revocations.json -device-root-pubkey <DEVICE_ROOT_PUBKEY> -account-service https://<ACCOUNT_HOST>:<ACCOUNT_PORT>"

# ===========================================================================
# STEP 3 — signed revocation bundles from the untrusted hop
# ===========================================================================
# BLOCKED on bacchus-payment#79: the signing ceremony that mints the revocations
# root. Until it has run, -revocations-root-pubkey stays unset and the two files
# from step 1 remain the only source, which is the state every coordinator ships
# in (issue #199, ADR-0017).
#
# This is ADDITIVE: it installs into the same in-memory list the two files above
# already populate, so both can run side by side during a migration. A source is
# an http(s) URL or a filesystem path an operator stages the bundle at. The two
# -*-state files hold each namespace's rollback floor — write access to one is
# equivalent to rolling that namespace back a generation, so they belong with the
# other secrets and never in a world-readable directory.
#
# A namespace whose own gate is off is started for nothing and says so
# (`signed revocations(device): … the device gate is not configured`), which
# bacchus-gate-check.sh reports as PARTIAL rather than as on.
#
#BACCHUS_COORDINATOR_GATES="-admission-authority relay,exit:<OPERATOR_ADMISSION_PUBKEY> -admission-authority client:<ACCOUNT_SERVICE_ADMISSION_PUBKEY> -admission-revocations /etc/bacchus/admission-revocations.json -device-revocations /etc/bacchus/device-revocations.json -device-root-pubkey <DEVICE_ROOT_PUBKEY> -account-service https://<ACCOUNT_HOST>:<ACCOUNT_PORT> -revocations-root-pubkey <REVOCATIONS_ROOT_PUBKEY> -device-revocations-source <DEVICE_REVOCATIONS_SOURCE> -admission-revocations-source <ADMISSION_REVOCATIONS_SOURCE> -device-revocations-state /etc/bacchus/device-revocations-state.json -admission-revocations-state /etc/bacchus/admission-revocations-state.json"

# ===========================================================================
# WHAT DOES NOT BELONG IN HERE
# ===========================================================================
# * The TURN password, ADVERTISE and TURN_PUBLIC_IP. Those are coordinator.env,
#   and that file holds a secret while this one holds public keys and addresses.
# * -policy-root-pubkey / -policy-source / -policy-state. Signed policy is
#   already configured on the live coordinator and it is the one control here
#   that fails CLOSED — moving it into this file would mean a mistyped gate line
#   stops the coordinator assigning work. Leave it in ExecStart.
# * -geoip, -operators, -country-overrides, -asn-table. Not gates: they change
#   what the directory says, not who may reach it.
#
# After any change: `systemctl restart bacchus-coordinator`, then read the gate
# report. Anchors are read once at startup; the revocation FILES are not, and are
# picked up without a restart.
